# PASS2 annotations, keyed by object or storage address (development file).
# [names]     $addr NAME group MEANING      variables (storage address)
# [fns]       $addr NAME PARAM MEANING      DEF FN (body address)
# [routines]  $addr TITLE                   comment on a routine's first line
# [lines]     $addr COMMENT                 comment on the line starting at addr
[names]
$66C9 RL   refs    REFERENCE LIST: FOR EACH LINE, FUNCTION AND DATA CHAIN A PAIR (LATEST REFERENCE, TARGET); FOR EACH NUMERIC VARIABLE OR CONSTANT ITS LATEST REFERENCE, STORAGE ADDRESS, ENTRY COUNT (NEGATIVE FOR AN INTEGER) AND ROUTINE ENTRIES; FOR EACH STRING VARIABLE OR ARRAY ITS LATEST REFERENCE AND ROUTINE ADDRESS
$666A NE   refs    NUMBER OF VALUES IN {RL}()
$6636 PZ   refs    SIZE OF {RL}() (5900 VALUES)
$663F PV   refs    VALUE TO ADD TO {RL}()
$66C5 GE   refs    {GE}(0) = LAST INDEX OF THE LINE AND FUNCTION PAIRS IN {RL}(), {GE}(1) = LAST INDEX OF THE NUMERIC ROUTINE GROUPS
$6662 I    refs    LOOP INDEX; ALSO THE INDEX IN {RL}()
$6664 G    refs    GAP OF THE SORT; ALSO A POWER OF TWO
$666E HO   refs    HIGHEST OPERATION OF A VARIABLE ROUTINE (-1 FOR NONE); ALSO AN INDEX IN THE SORT
$6634 RF   refs    ADDRESS OF A REFERENCE TO REPORT
$6643 LN   refs    NUMBER OF THE LINE THAT HOLDS IT
$665D Q    refs    A LINE NUMBER READ FROM THE LINE NUMBER FILE
$6621 E    symtab  SYMBOL TABLE ENTRY BEING WORKED ON
$666C J    symtab  CHAIN INDEX; ALSO A TYPE CODE
$6668 PR   symtab  PREVIOUS ENTRY (FOR UNLINKING); ALSO THE END OF THE CODE
$6670 TC   symtab  TYPE CODE AS STORED (50 HIGHER FOR COMMON, 47/49 FOR A REAL/INTEGER CONSTANT)
$6641 TY   symtab  TYPE CODE: -3 REAL, -2 STRING, -1 INTEGER, 19-21 ARRAYS
$663D AC   memory  ADDRESS BEING COUNTED (COMMON ALLOCATION), SIZE OF THE ROUTINE AREA, START OF THE PAGE BEING PATCHED
$6648 LV   memory  NEXT FREE ADDRESS FOR LOCAL VARIABLES
$664D PE   memory  LAST ADDRESS OF THE PROGRAM (CODE, CONSTANTS AND ROUTINES)
$6638 HM   memory  HIGHEST MEMORY ADDRESS USED
$6625 VS   memory  STORAGE ADDRESS OF THE VARIABLE; ALSO A VALUE READ FROM THE DATA TABLE
$667B MM   memory  MEMORY LAYOUT FOR THE HEADER: {MM}(0) LOCALS START-1, (1) END OF NUMERIC LOCALS, (2) END OF STRING LOCALS, (3) COMMON START-1, (4) END OF NUMERIC COMMON, (5) END OF STRING COMMON
$6658 S    memory  SCRATCH: A SIZE, A PRODUCT, THE FILE BASE
$6656 M    memory  SCRATCH: A LINE NUMBER, AN ELEMENT SIZE, AN OPERATION MASK
$6654 N    memory  SCRATCH: COUNT OF LINE NUMBER RECORDS READ, THE PASS OVER THE SYMBOLS (1 NUMBERS, 2 ARRAYS, 3 STRINGS), A KEY
$6677 C    memory  COMMON PASS (0 NUMBERS, 1 STRINGS); ALSO AN OPERATION OR DIMENSION INDEX
$6666 CE   output  ADDRESS OF THE LAST BYTE OF CODE
$6623 OB   output  BYTE TO WRITE TO THE OBJECT FILE (OR JUST READ)
$662A W    output  WORD TO WRITE
$662F R    output  WORD JUST READ
$6652 DI   output  WHICH DISPATCHER: 0 REAL, 1 STRING, 2 INTEGER, 3-5 REAL, STRING AND INTEGER ARRAYS, 6-10 LOOP STARTS AND NEXT
$6699 DS   output  DISPATCHER ADDRESSES: {DS}(I,0) LOW BYTE, {DS}(I,1) HIGH BYTE
$6672 FP   output  FILE POSITION OF A REFERENCE
$6679 OC   output  OPERATION NUMBER READ AT A REFERENCE
$94E3 Y$   report  THE ANSWER TO THE REPORT QUESTION
[fns]
$425F HB X  HIGH BYTE OF X
$426F LO X  LOW BYTE OF X
$427F UB X  THE ADDRESS STORED AS X (X+32767)
$4289 BI X  ADDRESS X AS IT IS STORED (X-32767)
$4296 TY X  TYPE CODE X WITHOUT THE COMMON MARK (X-50 ABOVE 21)
$42A9 LG X  THE NUMBER OF THE HIGHEST BIT SET IN X (FLOOR OF LOG2)
[routines]
$4206 PASS 2 OF THE COMPILER: CHECKS THAT EVERY LINE AND FUNCTION REFERRED TO EXISTS, WRITES THE CONSTANTS AND THE VARIABLE ROUTINES AFTER THE CODE, ALLOCATES COMMON AND LOCAL VARIABLES, REPLACES EVERY CHAINED REFERENCE IN THE OBJECT FILE BY ITS ADDRESS, WRITES THE HEADER AND REPORTS. PASS 1 LEFT EACH REFERENCE AS A LINK TO THE PREVIOUS ONE TO THE SAME SYMBOL, THE SYMBOL HOLDING THE LATEST
$42BC COLLECT A PAIR (LATEST REFERENCE, TARGET) FOR THE DATA CHAIN AND EVERY LINE SYMBOL
$4358 SORT THE LINE PAIRS BY LINE NUMBER (A SHELL SORT WITH AN ODD GAP IN PAIRS); THE DATA PAIR STAYS FIRST
$449C LOOK UP EACH LINE IN THE LINE NUMBER FILE (NUMBER AND ADDRESS OF EVERY LINE, IN ORDER), SO THE PAIR'S TARGET BECOMES THE LINE'S ADDRESS
$4594 FUNCTIONS: A PAIR (LATEST REFERENCE, ADDRESS OF THE BODY); A FUNCTION NEVER DEFINED IS AN ERROR
$466A REPORT WHERE THE REFERENCE AT {RF} IS (A FATAL ERROR); PAUSE IF ASKED
$4755 ANY FATAL ERROR ENDS THE COMPILATION HERE
$47A6 THE CONSTANT POOL, WRITTEN AFTER THE CODE: REAL CONSTANTS AS 5 BYTES, INTEGER CONSTANTS AS 2 (HIGH BYTE FIRST); AN INTEGER CONSTANT NEVER REFERRED TO IS DROPPED
$4906 COMMON ALLOCATION IN DECLARATION ORDER FROM THE START OF THE COMMON BLOCK: NUMBERS FIRST, THEN STRINGS
$4AC3 SIZE OF THE ROUTINE AREA: A NUMERIC ROUTINE 3 BYTES PER OPERATION USED PLUS 6, A STRING ROUTINE 10, AN ARRAY ROUTINE 9 + 2 PER DIMENSION
$4C27 LOCAL VARIABLES START AFTER THE ROUTINES, OR AT THE VARIABLES ADDRESS ASKED FOR
$4C79 THE DISPATCHERS THE ROUTINES JUMP TO: LIBRARY OFFSETS OF THE REAL, STRING AND INTEGER DISPATCHERS, THE THREE ARRAY DISPATCHERS, THE FOUR LOOP START ENTRIES AND NEXT
$4D09 THE ROUTINES AND THE LOCAL VARIABLES, IN THREE PASSES OVER THE SYMBOL TABLE: 1 NUMERIC VARIABLES, TEMPORARIES AND CONSTANTS, 2 NUMERIC ARRAYS, 3 STRING VARIABLES, TEMPORARIES AND ARRAYS. STORAGE IS ALLOCATED IN THE SAME ORDER
$4EE7 A NUMERIC VARIABLE'S ROUTINE: FOR EACH OPERATION USED, HIGHEST FIRST, LDX #OPERATION, EACH BUT THE FIRST PRECEDED BY $AD (THE LDA ABSOLUTE OPCODE SWALLOWS THE NEXT LDX, SO EACH ENTRY FALLS THROUGH); THEN LDY #HIGH, LDA #LOW OF THE STORAGE ADDRESS AND JMP TO THE DISPATCHER. NO OPERATION USED: NO ROUTINE
$502E A STRING VARIABLE'S ROUTINE: SEC, LDA #$18 (WHOSE $18 IS THE CLC OF THE SECOND ENTRY, 2 BYTES IN), LDY #HIGH, LDA #LOW, JMP TO THE STRING DISPATCHER: +0 LOADS, +2 STORES
$5046 ENTRY: A PAIR (LATEST REFERENCE, ROUTINE ADDRESS) AND SEC, LDA #$18
$5079 ENTRY: LDY #HIGH BYTE, LDA #LOW BYTE OF {VS}
$50A3 AN ARRAY'S ROUTINE: SEC, LDA #$18, JSR TO THE ARRAY DISPATCHER, THEN THE ARRAY'S ADDRESS, ITS NUMBER OF DIMENSIONS AND EACH DIMENSION (10 IF IT WAS NEVER DIMENSIONED): +0 LOADS AN ELEMENT, +2 GIVES ITS ADDRESS
$5181 THE END OF EACH PASS
$5217 BACKPATCHING: WORK THROUGH THE OBJECT FILE 256 BYTES AT A TIME FROM THE END; IN EACH PAGE FOLLOW EVERY CHAIN WHOSE LATEST REFERENCE LIES IN IT, REPLACING EACH LINK BY THE TARGET. BECAUSE LINKS ALWAYS POINT BACK, EVERY REFERENCE IS REACHED IN ITS PAGE
$55B3 THE HEADER, WRITTEN OVER THE 24 BYTES RESERVED AT THE START OF THE FILE: THE BINARY FILE'S ADDRESS AND LENGTH, JSR TO THE LIBRARY, THE FIRST DATA ITEM, THE HIGHEST ADDRESS, THE COMMON AND LOCAL BOUNDS, THE COMMON FLAG
$56B1 THE COMPILATION INFORMATION AND LINE NUMBER REFERENCE TABLE, IF ASKED FOR
$5BB1 THE END: PUT BACK THE SIX BYTES OF DOS THAT PASS 0 CHANGED
$5C37 ADD {PV} TO {RL}()
$5C58 ENTRY: JMP TO DISPATCHER {DI}; ENTRY AT 4720: THE OPCODE IN {OB}, THEN THE DISPATCHER'S ADDRESS
$5C85 WRITING: 4800 THE BYTE {OB} AND THE WORD {W} (A STORED ADDRESS); 4820 THE WORD {W} (A STORED ADDRESS); 4840 THE WORD {W} AS IT IS; 4880 THE BYTE {OB}
$5CDF FIND THE LINE THAT HOLDS ADDRESS {RF}: THE LAST LINE IN THE LINE NUMBER FILE THAT STARTS BELOW IT
$5D4A AT A VARIABLE REFERENCE IN THE FILE: READ ITS OPERATION BYTE INTO {OC} AND ITS LINK INTO {R}, THEN POSITION THE FILE FOR WRITING THE CALL OVER THEM
$5D74 AT A CHAINED WORD IN THE FILE: READ THE LINK INTO {R}, THEN POSITION THE FILE FOR WRITING OVER IT
$5DB6 READ A WORD (LOW BYTE FIRST) INTO {R}; 5300 RETURNS IT AS A STORED VALUE (-32767)
$5DDF POSITION THE OBJECT FILE FOR READING AT {FP}
$5E12 READ A BYTE INTO {OB}
$5E25 TOO LONG: THE PROGRAM, ITS VARIABLES OR THE REFERENCE LIST DO NOT FIT
$5E3F COMPILATION TERMINATED: DELETE THE OBJECT FILE
$5E9C REPORT A FATAL ERROR: END THE DIRECT FILE INPUT OR OUTPUT FIRST
$5EDD THE MOTOR SWITCHES OF THE SLOT DOS USED LAST
$5F0B STOP THE MOTOR AND CLOSE ALL FILES
[lines]
$4206 THE COPYRIGHT NOTICE; {Y$} IS THEN THE ANSWER STRING
$42BC THE LIST IS EMPTY
$42C8 THE DATA CHAIN FIRST: ITS LATEST LINK AND -32767 (0 AT THE END OF THE CHAIN)
$42DD OPEN THE LINE NUMBER FILE AND READ IT FROM THE START
$4301 KEEP THE DRIVE RUNNING
$4304 EVERY LINE NUMBER CHAIN
$431B EACH LINE SYMBOL: ITS LATEST REFERENCE AND ITS NUMBER (-32767)
$4358 ONLY THE DATA PAIR: NOTHING TO SORT OR LOOK UP
$4366 ONE LINE: NO SORT
$4374 THE GAP STARTS AT HALF THE PAIRS ...
$4386 ... ROUNDED TO WHOLE PAIRS ...
$43AC ... AND AN ODD NUMBER OF PAIRS
$43D2 COMPARE EACH PAIR WITH THE ONE A GAP FURTHER, MOVING THE SMALLER BACK AS FAR AS IT GOES
$448E A SMALLER GAP, DOWN TO ONE PAIR
$449C NO RECORDS READ YET
$44A2 EACH LINE REFERRED TO, IN ORDER
$44B9 THE WHOLE FILE READ WITHOUT FINDING IT
$44C7 THE NEXT RECORD: THE LINE NUMBER ...
$44CA (KEPT)
$44D0 ... AND THE ADDRESS
$44DC FOUND: THE PAIR'S TARGET IS THE LINE'S ADDRESS
$4505 NOT YET REACHED: READ ON
$4519 PASSED: NO SUCH LINE
$4553 THE LATEST REFERENCE TO IT
$455F READ THE LINE NUMBER FILE FROM THE START AGAIN
$4594 THE FOUR FUNCTION CHAINS
$45BC EACH FUNCTION: ITS LATEST REFERENCE ...
$45D1 ... AND THE ADDRESS OF ITS BODY
$4616 THE NAME FROM ITS HASH, 99*FIRST CHARACTER+SECOND
$466A THE LINE OF THE LATEST REFERENCE
$469D A FATAL ERROR
$46A6 WITHOUT THE PAUSE OPTION, GO ON AT ONCE
$46B1 STOP THE DRIVE MOTOR WHILE WAITING
$471A WAIT FOR A KEY; CONTROL-C ENDS THE COMPILATION
$475A CLOSE THE LINE NUMBER FILE
$47A6 THE CODE ENDS HERE
$47AF OPEN THE OBJECT FILE ...
$47CD ... AND POSITION IT AT ITS END
$47F3 EVERY CONSTANT CHAIN
$480D {PR} = THE PREVIOUS ENTRY
$4824 A REAL CONSTANT (ITS FIRST BYTE, THE EXPONENT, IS NOT NEGATIVE): WRITE ITS 5 BYTES AND REPLACE THEM BY ITS ADDRESS AND THE MARK 47 (A REAL CONSTANT)
$4887 AN INTEGER CONSTANT NEVER REFERRED TO: UNLINK IT
$48B6 ITS ADDRESS
$48C5 THE VALUE, HIGH BYTE FIRST
$48EF THE MARK 49: AN INTEGER CONSTANT
$4906 THE FIRST COMMON ADDRESS
$490C NUMBERS FIRST
$4912 FOR THE HEADER: THE ADDRESS BEFORE THE COMMON BLOCK
$4927 THE LIST OF COMMON DECLARATIONS AT {SY}(0)
$493E THE NEXT DECLARATION: ITS SYMBOL AND TYPE
$4959 IN THE STRING PASS ONLY STRINGS ...
$498E ... IN THE NUMERIC PASS ONLY NUMBERS
$49C6 THE NAME HASH IS REPLACED BY THE STORAGE ADDRESS ...
$49D5 ... AND THE TYPE IS MARKED COMMON (+50)
$49E7 2 BYTES AN ELEMENT FOR AN INTEGER OR A STRING, 5 FOR A REAL
$4A16 A SIMPLE VARIABLE
$4A30 AN ARRAY: THE PRODUCT OF (DIMENSION+1)
$4A82 THE END OF THE NUMERIC COMMON; NOW THE STRINGS
$4AAE THE END OF THE STRING COMMON
$4AC9 TEMPORARIES, VARIABLES AND CONSTANTS
$4AFD VARIABLES AND CONSTANTS
$4B0B A TEMPORARY: A STRING ONE (FREE OR IN USE) HAS A STRING ROUTINE
$4B3D 10 BYTES
$4B49 A VARIABLE OR CONSTANT: ITS TYPE
$4B6C AN ARRAY: 9 + 2 PER DIMENSION
$4BAA A NUMERIC ONE: NO OPERATION USED, NO ROUTINE
$4BC1 3 BYTES FOR EACH OPERATION BIT SET IN THE MASK ...
$4C1B ... AND 6 FOR LDY, LDA, JMP
$4C2C THE LINE AND FUNCTION PAIRS END HERE
$4C3B THE LOCALS START AFTER THE ROUTINES ...
$4C47 ... WHICH END THE PROGRAM
$4C50 OR AT THE ADDRESS ASKED FOR
$4C6A FOR THE HEADER
$4C79 $1764 REAL, $0C7F STRING, $0D06 INTEGER, $0B11 $0B14 $0B17 ARRAYS, $09E1 $09E5 $09DE $09E8 LOOP STARTS, $0A31 NEXT (FOR THE LIBRARY AT $0803)
$4CAE THE LIBRARY ADDRESS
$4CC8 EACH OFFSET PLUS THE LIBRARY ADDRESS
$4D1A THE CHAINS FROM 5: TEMPORARIES, VARIABLES, CONSTANTS
$4D34 NO TEMPORARIES IN THE ARRAY PASS
$4D54 CONSTANTS ONLY IN THE FIRST PASS
$4D85 CONTROL-C ENDS THE COMPILATION
$4DB4 A VARIABLE OR CONSTANT: ITS TYPE IS IN ITS FOURTH WORD
$4DD1 A TEMPORARY: IN ITS THIRD WORD
$4DDD A TEMPORARY STILL MARKED IN USE (TYPE-3)
$4DF7 THE TYPE WITHOUT THE COMMON MARK
$4E00 A LOCAL VARIABLE GOES AT THE NEXT FREE ADDRESS ...
$4E06 ... A CONSTANT OR COMMON VARIABLE HAS ITS ADDRESS ALREADY
$4E26 ARRAYS
$4E34 STRINGS IN THE THIRD PASS
$4E57 NUMBERS IN THE FIRST
$4E92 STRING ARRAYS IN THE THIRD PASS, NUMERIC ARRAYS IN THE SECOND
$4EE7 THE GROUP IN {RL}(): THE LATEST REFERENCE ...
$4EF6 ... THE STORAGE ADDRESS ...
$4F02 THE OPERATION MASK; {HO} = ITS HIGHEST BIT
$4F2E ... THE NUMBER OF ENTRIES+1 (NEGATIVE FOR AN INTEGER) ...
$4F65 ... AND FOR EACH OPERATION, HIGHEST FIRST, THE ADDRESS OF ITS ENTRY (-32767 IF NOT USED)
$4FAB $AD BEFORE EACH ENTRY BUT THE FIRST
$4FC8 LDX #OPERATION
$4FEB LDY #HIGH, LDA #LOW
$4FEE THE REAL DISPATCHER, OR THE INTEGER ONE
$4FF4 THE INTEGER DISPATCHER FOR AN INTEGER
$500B JMP
$500E A REAL TAKES 5 BYTES, AN INTEGER 2
$502E SEC, LDA #$18
$5031 LDY #HIGH, LDA #LOW
$5034 JMP TO THE STRING DISPATCHER
$503D A STRING VARIABLE IS A 2-BYTE POINTER
$505E SEC
$5064 LDA #
$5070 $18, THE CLC
$5079 LDY #
$5082 HIGH BYTE
$508E LDA #
$5097 LOW BYTE
$50A6 JSR TO THE ARRAY DISPATCHER: 3 REAL, 4 STRING, 5 INTEGER
$50B8 THE ARRAY'S ADDRESS
$50C7 THE NUMBER OF DIMENSIONS
$50F0 EACH DIMENSION, OR 10
$5122 THE NUMBER OF ELEMENTS
$5136 TIMES 2, OR 5 FOR A REAL ARRAY
$5159 CONSTANTS AND COMMON VARIABLES TAKE NO LOCAL STORAGE
$5167 THE NEXT FREE ADDRESS
$5170 THE VARIABLES MUST END BELOW $C000
$5186 AFTER THE FIRST PASS: THE NUMERIC ROUTINE GROUPS END HERE
$51A3 AFTER THE SECOND: THE NUMERIC LOCALS END HERE
$51C5 THE STRING LOCALS END HERE
$51D4 THE HIGHEST ADDRESS: THE END OF THE LOCALS, OF THE PROGRAM OR OF THE LIBRARY
$5217 KEEP THE END OF THE PROGRAM FOR THE HEADER
$521D {PC} COUNTS FROM 0 NOW, SO THE PATCHES DO NOT LOOK TOO LONG
$5226 THE OBJECT FILE IS WRITTEN AGAIN
$5241 THE FILE POSITION OF THE LAST BYTE OF CODE ...
$5250 ... ROUNDED DOWN TO A 256-BYTE PAGE OF THE FILE: {AC} = THE ADDRESS AT THE PAGE'S START
$525F END THE DIRECT OUTPUT
$5274 A DOT FOR EACH PAGE
$5281 THE DATA CHAIN: WHILE ITS LATEST LINK IS IN THIS PAGE ...
$5292 ... READ THE LINK AND POSITION THE FILE OVER IT
$52A1 THE LAST LINK GETS 0; EACH EARLIER ONE THE ADDRESS RIGHT AFTER THE LATER LINK: SO EACH DATA JMP GOES PAST ITS BLOCK AND EACH BLOCK'S LAST WORD POINTS AT THE NEXT BLOCK'S FIRST ITEM
$52CA (WRITE IT)
$52CD THIS LINK IS THE TARGET OF THE NEXT ONE BACK
$52DC FOLLOW THE CHAIN
$52EE NO LINE OR FUNCTION PAIRS
$52FF EACH LINE AND FUNCTION PAIR: WHILE ITS LATEST REFERENCE IS IN THIS PAGE, READ THE LINK AND WRITE THE TARGET'S ADDRESS OVER IT (THE JMP OR JSR BEFORE IT IS ALREADY THERE)
$535F NO NUMERIC ROUTINES
$5379 EACH NUMERIC VARIABLE OR CONSTANT
$5385 ITS LATEST REFERENCE IS BELOW THIS PAGE: THE NEXT GROUP
$5396 READ THE OPERATION AND THE LINK
$5399 FOLLOW THE CHAIN
$53A8 OPERATIONS 0-13: JSR TO THE ROUTINE'S ENTRY FOR THE OPERATION
$53F2 21-25: JSR TO THE LOOP START OR NEXT ENTRY, THEN THE VARIABLE'S ADDRESS IN THE TWO BYTES AFTER
$5439 20, AN AND / OR TEST: LDA OF THE VARIABLE ...
$5451 ... AND FOR AN INTEGER ORA OF ITS SECOND BYTE (THE THREE BYTES PASS 1 LEFT AFTER IT)
$5483 THE NEXT GROUP
$54B2 NO STRING OR ARRAY ROUTINES
$54CC EACH STRING VARIABLE OR ARRAY: JSR TO ITS ROUTINE, +2 FOR OPERATION 1 (STORE, OR AN ELEMENT'S ADDRESS)
$5543 CONTROL-C ENDS THE COMPILATION
$5569 THE PAGE BEFORE, UNTIL THE START OF THE FILE
$557D END THE DIRECT OUTPUT
$5595 WRITE AT THE START OF THE FILE
$55B3 THE BINARY FILE'S ADDRESS ...
$55BC ... AND LENGTH
$55CE JSR TO THE LIBRARY, WHICH COPIES THE HEADER TO PAGE ZERO
$55DD THE FIRST DATA ITEM (0 IF THERE IS NO DATA)
$5609 THE HIGHEST ADDRESS USED
$5612 THE COMMON BOUNDS
$5634 THE LOCAL BOUNDS
$5656 THE COMMON FLAG: 0 NONE OR DEFINE, 255 USE
$5676 CLOSE THE OBJECT FILE
$56E7 A LINE STARTING WITH CONTROL-D IS A DOS COMMAND: IT IS RUN, AND THE QUESTION IS ASKED AGAIN
$5799 NO: SKIP THE REPORT
$5889 THE PROGRAM START WAS NOT MOVED: NO COMMON
$5AD8 THREE COLUMNS OF LINE NUMBER AND ADDRESS
$5B13 READ THE LINE NUMBER FILE FROM THE START
$5B40 EACH RECORD: THE LINE NUMBER AND THE ADDRESS, THREE TO A ROW
$5B8F (THE NEXT COLUMN, OR A NEW ROW)
$5BE2 THE ADDRESSES AND ORIGINAL BYTES PASS 0 SAVED IN {DP}()
$5C16 OPEN AND CLOSE THE LINE NUMBER FILE
$5C43 ONLY 5900 VALUES FIT: TOO LONG
$5CA6 DOS'S OUTPUT STATE 4: EVERY CHARACTER GOES TO THE FILE; THE ROUTINE AT $0387 WRITES THE BYTE IN $0376
$5CC7 COUNT THE BYTE
$5CD0 THE OBJECT MAY NOT PASS $BFFF
$5CDF READ THE LINE NUMBER FILE FROM THE START
$5D0F EACH RECORD UNTIL ONE STARTS AT OR ABOVE THE ADDRESS
$5D4A THE FILE POSITION OF THE REFERENCE
$5D6B {OC} = THE OPERATION
$5D74 THE FILE POSITION
$5D83 DOS WRITE WITH A BYTE POSITION, RELATIVE TO THE FILE'S START
$5DC3 LOW BYTE ...
$5DCC ... AND HIGH BYTE
$5DEB DOS READ WITH A BYTE POSITION
$5E12 THE ROUTINE AT $037E READS A BYTE OF THE OPEN FILE INTO $0376
$5E42 CLOSE AND DELETE THE UNFINISHED OBJECT FILE
$5E9C DOS'S OUTPUT STATE 5, AND A CONTROL-D ENDS THE DIRECT FILE INPUT OR OUTPUT
$5EB7 TWO BELLS
$5EDD $C089 + SLOT*16 ($AA6A IS THE SLOT DOS USED LAST)
$5EFE KEEP THE DRIVE RUNNING
$5F0B STOP THE MOTOR
